ne Cipher","u":"ciphers/affine.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Substitution Hall","c":"Antiquity → 1800s · math-age","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"","s":"The affine cipher — a single linear formula E(x)=a·x+b mod 26 that generalises Caesar (a=1) and Atbash (a=25, b=25). Just 312 keys; trivially broken.","g":["ancient","the substitution hall","broken"]},{"t":"Agnes Meyer Driscoll","u":"ciphers/agnes-driscoll.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Interwar & WWII · USA","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Agnes Meyer Driscoll was the U.S. Navy's chief cryptanalyst for three decades, breaking Japanese naval codes and training generations of American codebreakers. Known as 'Miss Aggie' and 'Madame X', she was the most formidable cryptanalyst in prewar America.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Alberti Cipher Disk","u":"ciphers/alberti-disk.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Renaissance · 1467","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Index of Coincidence · Frequency analysis","s":"Alberti cipher disk — Leon Battista Alberti's 1467 rotating disk, the first true polyalphabetic device and the tool that began the cipher revolution.","g":["renaissance","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Ethiopian Geʼez Monastic Ciphers","u":"ciphers/amharic-ge-ez-ciphers.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"East Africa · 14th–19th c.","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Historical (low security)","s":"Ethiopian Orthodox monasteries used syllabary-based substitution ciphers in Ge'ez script to protect sacred texts and inter-monastery communications from the 14th century onward.","g":["medieval","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Arabic Nomenclators","u":"ciphers/arabic-nomenclators.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Abbasid Caliphate · ~850 CE","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Broken (by its own inventors)","s":"9th-century Abbasid Baghdad — al-Kindi's Risalah and the codebooks of the Arab cryptographic golden age, where frequency analysis was born.","g":["medieval","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Argenti Family Cipher","u":"ciphers/argenti.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Renaissance Italy · 1500s–1600s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"","s":"Matteo and Marcello Argenti served as papal cryptanalysts to seven popes. Their <em>Trattato in Cifra</em> codified the homophonic-nomenclator pattern that dominated European diplomacy for two centuries.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Arnold–André Book Cipher","u":"ciphers/arnold-andre.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"American Revolution · 1779–1780","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Compromised by capture (1780)","s":"The book cipher Benedict Arnold and Major John André used to plot the surrender of West Point. Each plaintext word located in a shared book (Blackstone’s <em>Commentaries</em>) and transmitted as the triple page.line.word.","g":["modern","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Atbash","u":"ciphers/atbash.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Ancient · ~500 BC","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but u