tatistical analysis of A/B distribution","s":"Bacon's bilateral cipher (1605) — Francis Bacon's 5-bit binary code that hides letters inside a two-typeface carrier, the direct ancestor of ASCII.","g":["renaissance","puzzle & novelty ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Bazeries Cipher","u":"ciphers/bazeries.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"1890s · France","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Layered frequency analysis","s":"Bazeries cylinder cipher — Etienne Bazeries' 1891 wheel cipher with 20 alphabet disks, an evolution later adopted by the US Army as the M-94 in 1922.","g":["19th century","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Beale Ciphers","u":"ciphers/beale.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"19th Century · published 1885","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Unsolved (Beale 1 & 3)","s":"Beale ciphers — three numerical ciphers published in an 1885 Lynchburg pamphlet, said to have been written in the 1820s and to point to a Virginia treasure. Only #2 was solved (Declaration of Independence key); #1 and #3 unsolved.","g":["19th century","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Beaufort Cipher","u":"ciphers/beaufort.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Revolution","c":"Victorian · 1857","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Kasiski Examination · Index of Coincidence","s":"Beaufort cipher (1857) — Sir Francis Beaufort's reciprocal Vigenère variant where encrypt and decrypt are the same operation. Broken by Kasiski examination.","g":["19th century","polyalphabetic revolution","broken"]},{"t":"Arne Beurling","u":"ciphers/beurling.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Sweden","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Swedish mathematician Arne Beurling broke the German Siemens T52 Geheimschreiber cipher in two weeks in 1940 — an achievement described by NSA historians as perhaps the greatest individual cryptanalytic feat in history.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Bible Code — Equidistant Letter Sequences","u":"ciphers/bible-code.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"1994 · Refuted 1999","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Debunked","s":"The Bible Code — equidistant letter sequences (ELS) claimed to hide prophecies in the Hebrew scriptures. A rigorous statistical refutation by McKay et al. (1999) showed the same method finds identical 'predictions' in War and Peace and Moby-Dick.","g":["digital","puzzle & novelty ciphers"]},{"t":"Bifid Cipher","u":"ciphers/bifid.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"1901","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Statistical analysis · Hill climbing","s":"Bifid cipher (1901) — Felix Delastelle's fractionation cipher that splits Polybius coordinates, transposes them across a period, and recombines.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Bill Tutte","u":"ciphers/bill-tutte.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Britain","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Bill Tutte was a Cambridge mathematician who reconstructed the complete logical structure of the Lorenz SZ40 cipher machine from ciphertext alone — without ever seeing the machine — enabling the creation of Colossus, the world's first programmable electronic computer.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Bletchley Park","u":"ciphers/bletchley-park.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · 1939–1945","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"","s":"Bletchley Park (Station X) was the British WWII signals intelligence headquarters where 10,000 people broke Enigma, Lorenz, and dozens of other Axis ciphers — arguably shortening the war by two years.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Book Cipher","u":"ciphers/book-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Field","c":"18th–20th c.","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Identifying the book — the Beale Cipher 2 was solved when the anonymous pamphleteer (published by James Ward, 1885) tried the Declaration of Independence","s":"Book Cipher (18th c.) — if both correspondents share the same book, the book itself is the key. Used by Benedict Arnold, Aaron Burr, and Beale.","g":["19th century","military & field","broken"]},{"t":"Cabinet Noir — Black Chambers of Europe","u":"ciphers/cabinet-noir.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Europe · 1550–1850","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"","s":"The European Black Chambers (cabinets noirs) were government departments that intercepted, deciphered, and resealed diplomatic mail from the 16th through 19th centuries — proto-signals-intelligence agencies.","g":["renaissance","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Caesar Cipher","u":"ciphers/caesar.html","k":"cipher","h":"Hall I","c":"Ancient Rome","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Frequency Analysis · Al-Kindi · 850 AD","s":"Caesar cipher — the simplest substitution cipher, used by Julius Caesar around 58 BC with a shift of 3. Broken by Al-Kindi's frequency analysis circa 850 AD.","g":["ancient","hall i","broken"]},{"t":"Cardano Autokey","u":"ciphers/cardano-autokey.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Polyalphabetic Era","c":"Italian Renaissance · 1550","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Easy (recovery cascade)","s":"Girolamo Cardano's 1550 autokey — prime the keystream with a single seed letter, then continue it with the plaintext itself. The first published self-keying scheme.","g":["renaissance","the polyalphabetic era","broken"]},{"t":"Cardano Grille","u":"ciphers/cardano-grille.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Transposition Hall","c":"Italian Renaissance · 1550","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Weak (steganography, not encryption)","s":"Girolamo Cardano's 1550 grille — a card with cut-out windows. Write the secret through the windows; fill the rest with innocuous text; the recipient overlays an identical grille to read.","g":["renaissance","the transposition hall","historical only"]},{"t":"Chaocipher","u":"ciphers/chaocipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Modern · 1918","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Published key-recovery attacks by Lasry, Rubin, Kopal and Wacker (2016).","s":"Chaocipher (1918) — John F. Byrne's two-disk dynamic cipher. Disclosed in 2010; published known-plaintext and in-depth ciphertext-only attacks recover its keys.","g":["wwi","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Che Guevara's VIC Variant","u":"ciphers/che-guevara.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Cold War · 1956–1967","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Bolivian Army capture (1967)","s":"Ernesto 'Che' Guevara used a modified VIC straddling-checkerboard cipher for guerrilla communications in Bolivia and the Congo, 1956–1967.","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Chinese Telegraph Code","u":"ciphers/chinese-telegraph.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Late Qing → PRC · 1881–1980s","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Public codebook (super-enciphered when secret)","s":"The 1881 Chinese Telegraph Code mapped 7,000+ Chinese characters to 4-digit code groups so they could ride the same Morse-style telegraph network as the Latin alphabet — and was super-enciphered for diplomatic and military traffic in the 20th century.","g":["modern","civil war gallery"]},{"t":"Cicada 3301","u":"ciphers/cicada-3301.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Internet era · 2012–2014","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Multi-stage ARG / recruitment puzzle","s":"An anonymous 2012–2014 internet recruitment puzzle that combined steganography, classical ciphers, GPS coordinates on physical posters, and a copy of the Liber Primus hand-illustrated in glyphic runes.","g":["digital","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Columnar Transposition","u":"ciphers/columnar.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"19th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Frequency analysis · Anagramming","s":"Columnar transposition — write plaintext under a keyword, then read columns in keyword order. Broken by Friedman-era multiple-anagram methods in the 1920s.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Commercial Telegraph Codebooks","u":"ciphers/commercial-codebooks.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Victorian → inter-war · 1870s–1930s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Public codebooks (compression, not secrecy)","s":"Commercial telegraph codebooks (ABC Code, Bentley’s, Lieber’s) were the workhorses of Victorian and Edwardian global business. Each English word or phrase mapped to a 5-letter pronounceable codeword — cheaper, denser, and incidentally a layer of confidentiality.","g":["modern","civil war gallery","broken"]},{"t":"Confedera