 spy networks in South America, and laid the foundations for what became the NSA.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Enigma Machine","u":"ciphers/enigma.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"WWII · 1918–1945","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Bombe machine · Crib attacks · Alan Turing · Bletchley Park","s":"Enigma (1923) — the German rotor cipher securing Wehrmacht and Kriegsmarine traffic. Broken by Polish mathematician Marian Rejewski in 1932.","g":["wwii","the unbreakable","broken"]},{"t":"Fialka M-125","u":"ciphers/fialka.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Cold War · 1956","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Never publicly broken by cryptanalysis in operational service; obsolete today.","s":"The Soviet Cold-War rotor machine. Ten rotors, half stepping forward and half backward, plus a punch-card key sheet — the answer to every weakness Soviet analysts saw in Enigma.","g":["cold war","mechanical cipher machines","historical only"]},{"t":"Field Hollers and Coding Songs","u":"ciphers/field-hollers.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Americas · 1619–1865","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Historical (social steganography)","s":"American enslaved workers used field hollers, work songs, and coded idiom as covert communication — \"Follow the Drinking Gourd\" encoding an escape route along the Underground Railroad.","g":["modern","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Four-Square Cipher","u":"ciphers/four-square.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Late 19th Century · ~1902","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Digram frequency analysis · simulated annealing","s":"Four-Square cipher (1902) — Delastelle's digram cipher using two keyed and two standard 5x5 squares. Disrupts Playfair frequencies but breaks to digrams.","g":["19th century","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Fractionated Morse Cipher","u":"ciphers/fractionated-morse.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"19th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Structural analysis of Morse patterns","s":"Fractionated Morse — converts text to Morse, regroups dots, dashes, and separators in trigrams, then substitutes via a keyed alphabet. Broken by trigram stats.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Freemason Pigpen Tradition","u":"ciphers/freemason-pigpen.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Early modern Europe · 17th–19th c.","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Fraternal symbol-substitution alphabet","s":"The geometric pigpen alphabet — fragments of grids and X-shapes — used by Freemasons, Rosicrucians, and the Hellfire Club from the 17th century onwards as a fraternal cipher and ritual marker.","g":["modern","cipher culture"]},{"t":"The GCHQ Trio — Ellis, Cocks, and Williamson","u":"ciphers/gchq-trio.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Cold War · GCHQ","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Invention Biography","s":"James Ellis, Clifford Cocks, and Malcolm Williamson of GCHQ independently invented public-key cryptography in 1970–1973 — but their work remained classified until 1997, some twenty years after Diffie, Hellman, and Rivest had received public credit.","g":["cold war","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Geheimschreiber T52 (Sturgeon)","u":"ciphers/geheimschreiber.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"WWII · 1932–1945","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Broken (Beurling, 1940)","s":"The Siemens T52 “Sturgeon” — a German WWII teleprinter cipher with ten irregularly-clocked wheels feeding an additive keystream and a per-character permutation. Broken by Arne Beurling and the Swedish FRA in summer 1940.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Poe's Gold-Bug Cipher","u":"ciphers/gold-bug.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Antebellum America · 1843","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Fictional substitution cipher","s":"Edgar Allan Poe's 1843 short story The Gold-Bug — the first widely read piece of fiction to teach cryptanalysis, complete with a worked decipherment of a buried-treasure cryptogram.","g":["modern","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Gravity Falls Cipher System","u":"ciphers/gravity-falls.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Animation · 2012–2016","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Pop Culture","s":"The Disney animated series Gravity Falls (2012–2016) hid a different cipher in every episode's credits — Caesar, Atbash, A1Z26 numeric, Vigenère — teaching real cryptography to a generation of children.","g":["digital","cipher culture"]},{"t":"The Great Cipher","u":"ciphers/great-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Baroque · ~1626","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Étienne Bazeries (1893): syllabic frequency analysis and contextual cribs.","s":"Great Cipher of Louis XIV (1626) — the Rossignol father-and-son syllabic nomenclator with delete-previous-letter traps. Broken by Bazeries in 1893.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Gronsfeld Cipher","u":"ciphers/gronsfeld.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Revolution","c":"17th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Kasiski · Only 10 possible shifts per position","s":"Gronsfeld cipher (1655) — a Vigenère variant