":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Jim Gillogly — hill climbing over column orders","s":"About 1,300 IRA cryptograms from the papers of chief of staff Moss Twomey, mostly 1926–1927, enciphered by columnar transposition and broken almost in their entirety by Jim Gillogly with hill-climbing software — published as Decoding the IRA (2008).","g":["interwar","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Jefferson Disk","u":"ciphers/jefferson-disk.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"1795 (Jefferson) · 1922 (M-94)","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Brute force · Probable word attack","s":"Jefferson disk cipher (1795) — Thomas Jefferson's 36-disk wheel cipher, rediscovered and adopted by the US Army as the M-94 in 1922. Solved by the late 20th c.","g":["interwar","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"JN-25","u":"ciphers/jn-25.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"World War II - 1939-1945","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Station HYPO and allied Pacific units","s":"JN-25 (1939-1945) - the Imperial Japanese Navy's superenciphered codebook system broken by Station HYPO and central to Midway intelligence.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Joan Clarke","u":"ciphers/joan-clarke.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Britain","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Joan Clarke was a Bletchley Park cryptanalyst who worked in Hut 8 alongside Alan Turing, making crucial contributions to breaking the Naval Enigma and to the development of the Bombe.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"David Kahn","u":"ciphers/kahn.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Modern · Journalist/Historian","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Cryptohistory Biography","s":"David Kahn's 1967 masterwork The Codebreakers was the first comprehensive history of cryptography — a book so thorough that the NSA tried to suppress its publication.","g":["modern","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Kama Sutra Cipher","u":"ciphers/kama-sutra.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Classical India · ~400 CE","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"","s":"The Mlecchita Vikalpa cipher from Vatsyayana's Kama Sutra (~400 CE) — a paired-letter substitution taught to lovers as one of the 64 arts.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Kerckhoffs's Principle","u":"ciphers/kerckhoffs.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Foundational · 1883","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Principle (not a cipher)","s":"Auguste Kerckhoffs's 1883 principle — 'A cipher should be secure even if everything about the system, except the key, is public knowledge' — is the bedrock of modern cryptographic design.","g":["19th century","modern cryptography"]},{"t":"KL-7 ADONIS","u":"ciphers/kl-7.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Cold War · 1952","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Never publicly broken by cryptanalysis in operational service; stolen keys exposed traffic.","s":"The KL-7 (ADONIS) was NATO’s standard cipher machine from 1952 to 1968. Eight rotors, seven of which stepped, on a re-entrant alphabet. Compromised by John Walker, not by cryptanalysis.","g":["cold war","mechanical cipher machines","historical only"]},{"t":"Kryha","u":"ciphers/kryha.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Inter-war commercial · 1924","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Broken (Friedman, 1933)","s":"Alexander von Kryha’s 1924 pocket cipher — a clockwork-driven mixed alphabet wheel sold to banks and diplomats as unbreakable, then broken in two hours by William Friedman.","g":["interwar","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"ARG Ciphers","u":"ciphers/krypto-arg.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Web 2.0 · 2001–present","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Promotional / collaborative cipher hunts","s":"Alternate-reality games — from The Beast (2001) to Year Zero (2007) and beyond — built on the assumption that players will recognise, transcribe, and crack classical ciphers across forums, phone calls, and physical artifacts.","g":["digital","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Kryptos","u":"ciphers/kryptos.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Modern · 1990","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"K1–K3 Broken · K4 Unsolved","s":"Kryptos (1990) — Jim Sanborn's CIA-courtyard sculpture in four sections. K1, K2, K3 solved by 1999 (keyed Vigenère and transposition); K4 still unsolved.","g":["digital","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"George Lasry","u":"ciphers/lasry.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Modern · Computational","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Living Legend","s":"George Lasry is a computational cryptographer who has solved more historically unsolved cipher manuscripts in the past decade than anyone alive — including the Copiale Cipher and multiple Zodiac Killer messages.","g":["modern","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Latin American Telegraphic Codebooks","u":"ciphers/latin-american-codebooks.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Americas · 1870s–1940s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Low (commercial, not military)","s":"The commercial telegraph boom of 1870–1940 spawned a rich family of Latin American codebooks — from the Código Comercial Mexicano to the Código Telegráfico Argentino — that shaped 20th-century trade.","g":["modern","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Leo Marks","u":"ciphers/leo-marks.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Britain","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Leo Marks was the SOE's head of cryptography in WWII, who replaced vulnerable poem codes with one-time pads for field agents, dramatically reducing agent capture rates. He wrote the poem 'The Life That I Have' for agent Violette Szabo.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Lorenz Cipher","u":"ciphers/lorenz.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"WWII · Germany · 1940","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Bill Tutte · Known plaintext · Colossus computer (1944)","s":"Lorenz SZ40 cipher (1940) — the German High Command teleprinter cipher with twelve pin-wheels. Broken by Bill Tutte in 1942; industrialised by Colossus in 1944.","g":["wwii","the unbreakable","broken"]},{"t":"M-94 / CSP-488","u":"ciphers/m-94.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"WWI → WWII tactical · 1922–1943","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Tactical only","s":"The M-94 (Army) and CSP-488 (Navy) — 25 brass disks on a spindle, each carrying a mixed alphabet. Jefferson’s 1795 idea, mass-produced for WWI tactical traffic and used into 1942.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"M-209 (Hagelin C-38)","u":"ciphers/m209.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"World War II · 1940","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Strong (tactical)","s":"M-209 cipher machine (1940) — the US Army's portable Hagelin C-38 with six co-prime pin-wheels and a 27-bar lug cage. Broken in the field in WWII.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Mary Queen of Scots — The Castelnau Letters","u":"ciphers/mary-stuart-castelnau-letters.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"1586","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Solved 2022 — after 436 years","s":"The 1586 Castelnau letters by Mary Queen of Scots — a 50,000-character cipher solved by George Lasry, Norbert Biermann, and Satoshi Tomokiyo in 2022, revealing undocumented French diplomatic intelligence.","g":["renaissance","hall of codebreakers","broken"]},{"t":"Mavis Batey","u":"ciphers/mavis-batey.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Britain","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Mavis Batey (née Lever) was a Bletchley Park cryptanalyst who broke the Italian naval Enigma in 1941, providing the intelligence behind the Battle of Cape Matapan — the Royal Navy's greatest WWII Mediterranean victory.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"McCormick Notes","u":"ciphers/mccormick.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Late 20th-c. United States · 1999","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Unsolved homicide-evidence cipher","s":"Two handwritten notes found in the pockets of murder victim Ricky McCormick in 1999 — declared 'unbreakable' by the FBI's Cryptanalysis and Racketeering Records Unit in 2011.","g":["digital","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Microdot Steganography","u":"ciphers/microdot.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Modern · 1941–1980s","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Physical discovery — the FBI photographically enlarged suspicious punctuation","s":"The microdot — a photograph reduced to the size of a typographic period — was the most effective covert communication tool of the 20th century, used by the Abwehr, KGB, and CIA from WWII through the Cold War.","g":["wwii","military & spy ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"MIT Mystery Hunt","u":"ciphers/mit-mystery-hunt.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"MIT · 1981–present","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Annual collaborative puzzle hunt","s":"An annual three-day puzzle marathon at MIT (since 1981) where teams solve dozens of layered cryptographic, linguistic, and lateral-thinking puzzles to find a hidden coin somewhere on campus.","g":["cold war","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Monoalphabetic Substitution","u":"ciphers/monoalphabetic.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Medieval · ~800 AD","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Al-Kindi · Frequency Analysis · 850 AD","s":"Monoalphabetic substitution — replace each letter with a fixed substitute. Foundation of all substitution ciphers; broken by Al-Kindi's frequency analysis ~850.","g":["medieval","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Morse Code","u":"ciphers/morse.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Telegraph age · 1840s →","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Encoding, not encryption","s":"International Morse code — the dot-dash telegraph alphabet that made the wired world possible and provided the carrier signal for a century of military and amateur ciphers.","g":["victorian","world origins of cryptography"]},{"t":"National Treasure","u":"ciphers/national-treasure.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Fiction · 2004","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Pop Culture","s":"The 2004 Disney film National Treasure sent audiences hunting for cipher clues on the US dollar bill, the Liberty Bell, and Independence Hall — all real historical sites.","g":["digital","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Navajo Code Talkers","u":"ciphers/navajo-code-talkers.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"WWII · 1942–1945","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Never publicly broken in wartime service; a historical military code.","s":"Navajo Code (1942-1945) — a constructed military vocabulary spoken by 420 Marine Code Talkers. Never broken in wartime; declassified in 1968 after long use.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","historical only"]},{"t":"Nihilist Cipher","u":"ciphers/nihilist.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"1880s · Russia","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Frequency analysis of digit pairs · Kasiski-equivalent","s":"Nihilist cipher (1880s) — the Russian revolutionary classic adding Polybius numbers to a repeating keyword's Polybius numbers by plain addition. Broken early 1900s.","g":["19th century","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Nomenclator","u":"ciphers/nomenclator.html","k":"cipher","h":"Substitution Era","c":"Renaissance · 1400s–1800s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Black Chambers (Vienna, Paris, London) c. 1700–1850 by sheer intercept volume","s":"Nomenclator (1400s) — The 400-year diplomatic compromise: a small substitution alphabet plus a large code dictionary. Hall II of The Cipher Museum.","g":["renaissance","substitution era","broken"]},{"t":"Null Cipher","u":"ciphers/null-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Transposition Hall","c":"Antiquity → present","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Weak (concealment, not encryption)","s":"The null cipher — hide the secret in plain sight by spelling it out with the first letters of an innocuous message. Used by spies, prisoners, and Bacon, since at least the Roman empire.","g":["ancient","the transposition hall","historical only"]},{"t":"One-Time Pad","u":"ciphers/one-time-pad.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"1882 · Theoretical","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"Requires a secret, truly random, message-length pad used once.","s":"One-Time Pad (Miller 1882 / Mauborgne 1917) — the only cipher mathematically unbreakable when the key is truly random, key-length, and never reused (Shannon).","g":["theoretical","the unbreakable","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"Patterson's Cipher for Jefferson","u":"ciphers/patterson-jefferson-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"1801","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Solved 2007 — after 206 years","s":"Robert Patterson's 1801 cipher for Thomas Jefferson — text written down the lines, cut into sections of at most nine, the lines shuffled and padded with arbitrary letters. Unread for 206 years until Lawren Smithline solved it in 2007 by dynamic programming over digraph frequencies.","g":["modern","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Phaistos Disc","u":"ciphers/phaistos-disc.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Bronze Age Crete · ~1700 BCE","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Undeciphered script","s":"A 1700 BCE Cretan terracotta disc stamped with 45 distinct pictograms — the world's oldest 'movable type' and one of its most enduring undeciphered scripts.","g":["ancient","unsolved ciphers"]},{"t":"Pigpen Cipher","u":"ciphers/pigpen.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"18th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Symbol frequency analysis · Pattern recognition","s":"Pigpen cipher (~1700) — Freemason geometric substitution where each letter becomes the lines and dots of its grid cell. Trivial once the glyph table is seen.","g":["medieval","puzzle & novelty ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Playfair Cipher","u":"ciphers/playfair.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Victorian · 1854","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Digraph statistics · hill climbing","s":"Playfair cipher (1854) — Charles Wheatstone's keyed 5x5 digram cipher promoted by Lord Playfair. Used by the British in WWI; broken by digram analysis.","g":["19th century","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Polybius Square","u":"ciphers/polybius.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Ancient Greece · ~150 BC","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Frequency analysis","s":"Polybius square (~150 BC) — ancient Greek 5x5 grid mapping each letter to a row-column digit pair. Foundation for Bifid, Trifid, Nihilist, ADFGX, and tap codes.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Porta Cipher","u":"ciphers/porta.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Revolution","c":"Renaissance · 1563","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Index of Coincidence · Statistical analysis","s":"Porta cipher (1563) — della Porta's reciprocal polyalphabetic table where each key letter swaps two halves of the alphabet. Falls to Kasiski just like Vigenère.","g":["renaissance","polyalphabetic revolution","broken"]},{"t":"Purple (97-shiki ōbun injiki)","u":"ciphers/purple.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"World War II · 1939–1945","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Frank Rowlett, Genevieve Grotjan & the US SIS · 20 September 1940","s":"Purple machine (1939) — Japan's Type 97 stepping-switch cipher for diplomatic traffic. Broken on 20 September 1940 by Frank Rowlett and the US Army SIS team.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Rail Fence Cipher","u":"ciphers/rail-fence.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"19th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Brute force · Trivial keyspace","s":"Rail Fence cipher — write the message in a zigzag across N rails, then read off rail by rail. Trivial to break: brute-force every rail count from 2 to about 10.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Red Army Faction One-Time Pad","u":"ciphers/red-army-faction.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Cold War · 1970–1998","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"","s":"The West German Red Army Faction (RAF/Baader-Meinhof) used KGB-supplied one-time pads for communications — materials discovered in 1993 after German reunification archive access.","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Red (Type A)","u":"ciphers/red-type-a.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Interwar to WWII - 1931-1938","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Broken by SIS","s":"Red (Type A) (1931-1938) - the Japanese diplomatic stepping-switch predecessor to Purple and the cryptanalytic bridge to MAGIC.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Joseph Rochefort","u":"ciphers/rochefort.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Pacific","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Commander Joseph Rochefort led Station HYPO and broke JN-25 in 1942, providing the intelligence that won the Battle of Midway and turned the tide of the Pacific War.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"The Rosetta Stone","u":"ciphers/rosetta-stone.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Ptolemaic Egypt · 196 BCE","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Solved 1822 (Champollion)","s":"The Rosetta Stone (196 BCE) — the trilingual decree that unlocked Egyptian hieroglyphs after fourteen centuries of silence, deciphered by Champollion in 1822.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography"]},{"t":"ROT13","u":"ciphers/rot13.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1980s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Knowing the system","s":"ROT13 (~1983, Usenet) — the self-inverse Caesar with shift 13, designed as a spoiler obscurer rather than a cipher. One operation toggles text in and out.","g":["19th century","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"RSA","u":"ciphers/rsa.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1977","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"","s":"The first practical public-key cryptosystem — one key encrypts, a different key decrypts, and you can publish the encrypting key in the phone book.","g":["cold war","modern cryptography","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"Running Key Cipher","u":"ciphers/running-key.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Revolution","c":"19th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Source text identification · Statistical analysis","s":"Running Key cipher (19th c.) — a Vigenère variant using a long passage of natural text as the key. Broken by Friedman with high-probability-trigram dragging.","g":["19th century","polyalphabetic revolution","broken"]},{"t":"Sator Square","u":"ciphers/sator-square.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Roman Empire · 1st–4th c. CE","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Palindromic word square / hidden acrostic","s":"A 1st-century Roman five-letter palindromic word square — SATOR · AREPO · TENET · OPERA · ROTAS — readable forwards, backwards, up, and down. Every element of the square also rearranges into PATER NOSTER + A and O.","g":["ancient","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Scytale","u":"ciphers/scytale.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Sparta · ~500 BC","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Brute force — try different rod diameters","s":"Scytale (~500 BC) — the Spartan transposition device that wraps a leather strip around a rod of fixed diameter. Broken trivially by trying every rod width.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"SHA-256","u":"ciphers/sha256.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 2001","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"","s":"SHA-256 (2001) — a 256-bit fingerprint for any input; the verification engine of Git, Bitcoin, TLS certificates, and software integrity worldwide.","g":["digital","modern cryptography","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"Shamir's Secret Sharing","u":"ciphers/shamir-secret-sharing.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1979","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"","s":"Shamir's Secret Sharing (1979) — split a secret so any k of n holders can rebuild it and any k−1 learn absolutely nothing.","g":["cold war","modern cryptography","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"Shugborough Inscription","u":"ciphers/shugborough.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Georgian England · ~1748–1763","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Unsolved monument inscription","s":"An 18th-century English garden monument bearing the cryptic letters O · U · O · S · V · A · V · V — debated for 250 years and never definitively cracked.","g":["modern","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"SIGABA (ECM Mark II)","u":"ciphers/sigaba.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Machines","c":"WWII · 1940","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Never publicly broken by cryptanalysis in wartime service; obsolete today.","s":"SIGABA (ECM Mark II) (1940) — The American WWII rotor machine the Axis never broke — fifteen rotors, irregular stepping. Hall VII of The Cipher Museum.","g":["wwii","mechanical machines","historical only"]},{"t":"SIGSALY — The Encrypted Speech System","u":"ciphers/sigsaly.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"WWII · 1943–1946","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"","s":"SIGSALY (1943–1946) was the world's first perfectly secure voice encryption system — a 50-ton machine using vinyl phonograph records as one-time pads to protect Roosevelt-Churchill phone calls during WWII.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","historical only"]},{"t":"Slidex","u":"ciphers/slidex.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"WWII & Cold War · 1943–1980s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Tactical (intentionally weak)","s":"Slidex (1943) — the British Army's front-line tactical code card. A 204-cell vocabulary grid plus two sliding coordinate strips. Fast for a platoon signaller; read by German units within hours.","g":["wwii","civil war gallery","broken"]},{"t":"Solitaire / Pontifex","u":"ciphers/solitaire.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"Modern · 1999","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"","s":"Solitaire / Pontifex (1999) — Bruce Schneier's hand cipher driven by a 54-card deck (written for Cryptonomicon). Crowley showed a ~1/22.5 keystream bias.","g":["digital","the unbreakable","historical only"]},{"t":"Somerton Man Code","u":"ciphers/somerton-man.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Post-WWII Australia · 1948","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Unsolved Cold-War-era inscription","s":"An unidentified man found dead on an Adelaide beach in 1948 with the word 'Tamám Shud' in his pocket — and a page of letters that may or may not be a cipher.","g":["wwii","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Stager Cipher","u":"ciphers/stager.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Union · 1861","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Never broken in wartime — superseded after the war","s":"Stager cipher (1861) — Anson Stager's Union Army route cipher that transposes whole words and salts them with code names. Never broken in the Civil War.","g":["wwi","civil war gallery","broken"]},{"t":"Station HYPO — Pearl Harbor to Midway","u":"ciphers/station-hypo.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · 1940–1942","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Broken (JN-25)","s":"Station HYPO at Pearl Harbor was the US Navy signals intelligence unit that broke JN-25 in 1942 and gave Admiral Nimitz advance warning of the Japanese fleet's target at Midway — turning the tide of the Pacific War.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Straddling Checkerboard","u":"ciphers/straddling-checkerboard.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Late 19th – 20th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Weak (alone) · Strong (in VIC)","s":"Straddling Checkerboard (1930s) — the Soviet variable-length digit substitution: common letters get one digit, rare letters two. The substitution layer of VIC.","g":["19th century","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Tap Code","u":"ciphers/tap-code.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"Korean War · Vietnam War","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Frequency analysis · Grid deduction","s":"Tap Code (1965) — the 5x5 Polybius square tapped through walls as row-then-column knocks. Used by US POWs in the Hanoi Hilton during the Vietnam War.","g":["cold war","puzzle & novelty ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Trifid Cipher","u":"ciphers/trifid.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"1902","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Statistical analysis","s":"Trifid cipher (1902) — Felix Delastelle's three-dimensional 3x3x3 extension of the Bifid that fractionates letters into trits and shuffles them across a period.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Trithemius Progressive Cipher","u":"ciphers/trithemius.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Polyalphabetic Era","c":"German Renaissance · 1518","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Easy","s":"Johannes Trithemius's progressive shift cipher (Polygraphia, 1518) — the first published polyalphabetic, where every successive letter is shifted one further than the last.","g":["renaissance","the polyalphabetic era","broken"]},{"t":"Two-Square Cipher","u":"ciphers/two-square.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Late 19th Century · ~1902","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Digram frequency · same-row leak","s":"Two-Square cipher (late 19th c.) — a lighter Delastelle variant using only two keyed 5x5 squares. Many digrams self-encode, leaking the key squares.","g":["19th century","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Typex","u":"ciphers/typex.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Machines","c":"WWII · 1937","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Never publicly broken by cryptanalysis in wartime service; obsolete today.","s":"Typex (1937) — Britain's improved Enigma — five rotors, no plugboard, used at Bletchley to read its own intercepts. Hall VII of The Cipher Museum.","g":["wwii","mechanical machines","historical only"]},{"t":"VENONA Project","u":"ciphers/venona.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"Cold War · 1943–1980","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Partial break (key reuse exploit)","s":"VENONA (1943–1980) was the US Army codebreaking effort that decrypted thousands of Soviet intelligence cables by exploiting key reuse in one-time pads — revealing Julius Rosenberg, Kim Philby, and the Cambridge Five.","g":["wwii","the unbreakable","broken"]},{"t":"Vernam Cipher","u":"ciphers/vernam.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"1917","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"Secure only under OTP conditions: a secret, truly random, message-length pad used once. XOR alone or pad reuse is not secure.","s":"Vernam cipher (1917) — Gilbert Vernam's AT&T XOR teleprinter patent, the direct ancestor of the One-Time Pad and every modern stream cipher including ChaCha20.","g":["wwi","the unbreakable","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"VIC Cipher","u":"ciphers/vic.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Cold War · 1950s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Never broken by cryptanalysis — exposed by defector (1957)","s":"VIC cipher (1953) — Reino Häyhänen's KGB hand cipher: straddling checkerboard, double transposition, date-driven keystream. Broken in 1957 after his defection.","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Vietnamese Underground Codes","u":"ciphers/vietnamese-underground.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Modern · 1940s–1975","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"SIGINT (NSA/ARVN), captured materials","s":"The Viet Minh and NLF used a system of keyed monoalphabetic substitution and prearranged signal codes during the Indochina and Vietnam Wars (1940s–1975).","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Vigenère Cipher","u":"ciphers/vigenere.html","k":"cipher","h":"Hall III","c":"Renaissance · 1553","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Babbage ~1854 · Kasiski 1863","s":"Vigenère cipher (1553) — the polyalphabetic shift driven by a repeating keyword, called le chiffre indéchiffrable for 300 years. Broken by Kasiski in 1863.","g":["renaissance","hall iii","broken"]},{"t":"The Voynich Manuscript","u":"ciphers/voynich.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Early 15th c. · Carbon-dated 1404–1438","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"No one. Every published \"solution\" has been refuted.","s":"Voynich Manuscript (~1400s) — a 240-page illustrated codex in an unknown script (Beinecke MS 408). Has resisted cipher and linguistic analysis for 600 years.","g":["renaissance","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Wadsworth Cipher","u":"ciphers/wadsworth-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"1817 · USA","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Ciphertext-only stochastic hill climbing (2020)","s":"Wadsworth's cipher device (1817) — Colonel Decius Wadsworth's two geared disks, 33 outer positions against 26 inner letters, the first working progressive cipher machine. Broken by a ciphertext-only hill-climbing attack in 2020.","g":["19th century","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Wallis Ciphers","u":"ciphers/wallis-ciphers.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"English Civil War · 1642–1651","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Royalist nomenclators — broken by Wallis","s":"John Wallis of Oxford broke Royalist nomenclators for Parliament during the English Civil War, then served every English government for 50 years. Originator of professional English state cryptanalysis.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Wheatstone Cryptograph","u":"ciphers/wheatstone.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Victorian London · 1867","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Weak (1867 standards)","s":"Charles Wheatstone's 1867 cryptograph — two geared clock dials whose hands sweep an outer plain alphabet and an inner mixed alphabet, generating a polyalphabetic ciphertext one click at a time.","g":["victorian","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Herbert Yardley","u":"ciphers/yardley.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Interwar · USA","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Herbert Osborne Yardley founded America's Black Chamber in 1919, broke Japanese diplomatic codes at the 1921 Washington Naval Conference, and then infamously published the secrets in his 1931 bestseller.","g":["interwar","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Zero-Knowledge Proofs","u":"ciphers/zero-knowledge-proofs.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1985","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"","s":"Zero-knowledge proofs (1985) — convince someone a statement is true while revealing nothing beyond its truth. Run the Schnorr protocol and its simulator.","g":["cold war","modern cryptography","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"Zimmermann Telegram","u":"ciphers/zimmermann.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"WWI · January 1917","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Broken (Room 40)","s":"The decoded German telegram of January 1917 that brought the United States into WWI. A two-stage codebook cipher (0075 then 13040), broken by Britain’s Room 40 and surfaced through one of history’s most consequential intelligence operations.","g":["wwi","civil war gallery","broken"]},{"t":"Zodiac Cipher","u":"ciphers/zodiac.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"1969 · Solved 2020","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Oranchak, Blake & Van Eycke (2020)","s":"Zodiac killer ciphers (1969) — homophonic substitution as in Z-408 (broken in 1969 by the Hardens) and Z-340 (broken in 2020 by Oranchak, Blake, and Van Eycke).","g":["cold war","puzzle & novelty ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"World Origins of Cryptography","u":"halls/ancient.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall I · World Origins · ~1900 BCE - ~1350 CE","b":"—","s":"World origins of cryptography from ancient Egypt and Hebrew Atbash through Greek, Roman, Indian, and Arabic traditions.","g":["hall","ancient"]},{"t":"The American Civil War Gallery","u":"halls/civil-war.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall VI · Special Exhibition · 1861 – 1865","b":"—","s":"Civil War cryptography: the Stager cipher, Confederate Vigenère, and dictionary codes from the American Civil War.","g":["hall"]},{"t":"Hall of Codebreakers","u":"halls/codebreakers.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall X · The Human Stories","b":"—","s":"Twenty-one moments when human ingenuity broke what mathematics could not. From Al-Kindi (850 AD) to the Copiale and Chaocipher reconstructions of 2010-2014.","g":["hall","ancient","renaissance","19th century","wwi","cold war"]},{"t":"Cipher Culture","u":"halls/culture.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall XIII · Cipher Culture","b":"—","s":"Cryptography in art, music, literature, espionage fiction, and games — Bach's BACH motif