ne Cipher","u":"ciphers/affine.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Substitution Hall","c":"Antiquity → 1800s · math-age","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"","s":"The affine cipher — a single linear formula E(x)=a·x+b mod 26 that generalises Caesar (a=1) and Atbash (a=25, b=25). Just 312 keys; trivially broken.","g":["ancient","the substitution hall","broken"]},{"t":"Agnes Meyer Driscoll","u":"ciphers/agnes-driscoll.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Interwar & WWII · USA","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Agnes Meyer Driscoll was the U.S. Navy's chief cryptanalyst for three decades, breaking Japanese naval codes and training generations of American codebreakers. Known as 'Miss Aggie' and 'Madame X', she was the most formidable cryptanalyst in prewar America.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Alberti Cipher Disk","u":"ciphers/alberti-disk.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Renaissance · 1467","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Index of Coincidence · Frequency analysis","s":"Alberti cipher disk — Leon Battista Alberti's 1467 rotating disk, the first true polyalphabetic device and the tool that began the cipher revolution.","g":["renaissance","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Ethiopian Geʼez Monastic Ciphers","u":"ciphers/amharic-ge-ez-ciphers.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"East Africa · 14th–19th c.","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Historical (low security)","s":"Ethiopian Orthodox monasteries used syllabary-based substitution ciphers in Ge'ez script to protect sacred texts and inter-monastery communications from the 14th century onward.","g":["medieval","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Arabic Nomenclators","u":"ciphers/arabic-nomenclators.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Abbasid Caliphate · ~850 CE","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Broken (by its own inventors)","s":"9th-century Abbasid Baghdad — al-Kindi's Risalah and the codebooks of the Arab cryptographic golden age, where frequency analysis was born.","g":["medieval","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Argenti Family Cipher","u":"ciphers/argenti.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Renaissance Italy · 1500s–1600s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"","s":"Matteo and Marcello Argenti served as papal cryptanalysts to seven popes. Their <em>Trattato in Cifra</em> codified the homophonic-nomenclator pattern that dominated European diplomacy for two centuries.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Arnold–André Book Cipher","u":"ciphers/arnold-andre.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"American Revolution · 1779–1780","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Compromised by capture (1780)","s":"The book cipher Benedict Arnold and Major John André used to plot the surrender of West Point. Each plaintext word located in a shared book (Blackstone’s <em>Commentaries</em>) and transmitted as the triple page.line.word.","g":["modern","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Atbash","u":"ciphers/atbash.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Ancient · ~500 BC","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Inspection (it is its own inverse)","s":"Atbash — the ancient Hebrew reflection cipher (A<->Z, B<->Y, C<->X), self-inverse and used in the Book of Jeremiah (25:26, 51:41) for SHESHACH.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Autokey","u":"ciphers/autokey.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Frontier","c":"Renaissance · 1586","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Friedman's index of coincidence + autokey-aware crib dragging (1920s)","s":"Autokey (1586) — Vigenère's self-extending key, the message becomes its own key. Effectively unbreakable until Friedman's 1920s statistical attack.","g":["renaissance","polyalphabetic frontier","broken"]},{"t":"The Babington Plot Cipher","u":"ciphers/babington.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Elizabethan · 1586","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Thomas Phelippes, working for Sir Francis Walsingham · within days of interception","s":"Babington Plot nomenclator (1586) — the cipher that condemned Mary, Queen of Scots, broken in days by Walsingham's cryptographer Thomas Phelippes.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Bach's B-A-C-H Motif","u":"ciphers/bach-motif.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Baroque Germany · 1750","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Musical cryptogram","s":"The four notes B♭ – A – C – B♮ spell 'BACH' in German musical notation — a self-signature Bach embedded in his final unfinished fugue.","g":["modern","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Bacon's Cipher","u":"ciphers/bacon.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"1605","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Pattern recognition · Statistical analysis of A/B distribution","s":"Bacon's bilateral cipher (1605) — Francis Bacon's 5-bit binary code that hides letters inside a two-typeface carrier, the direct ancestor of ASCII.","g":["renaissance","puzzle & novelty ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Bazeries Cipher","u":"ciphers/bazeries.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"1890s · France","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Layered frequency analysis","s":"Bazeries cylinder cipher — Etienne Bazeries' 1891 wheel cipher with 20 alphabet disks, an evolution later adopted by the US Army as the M-94 in 1922.","g":["19th century","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Beale Ciphers","u":"ciphers/beale.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"19th Century · published 1885","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Unsolved (Beale 1 & 3)","s":"Beale ciphers — three numerical ciphers published in an 1885 Lynchburg pamphlet, said to have been written in the 1820s and to point to a Virginia treasure. Only #2 was solved (Declaration of Independence key); #1 and #3 unsolved.","g":["19th century","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Beaufort Cipher","u":"ciphers/beaufort.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Revolution","c":"Victorian · 1857","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Kasiski Examination · Index of Coincidence","s":"Beaufort cipher (1857) — Sir Francis Beaufort's reciprocal Vigenère variant where encrypt and decrypt are the same operation. Broken by Kasiski examination.","g":["19th century","polyalphabetic revolution","broken"]},{"t":"Arne Beurling","u":"ciphers/beurling.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Sweden","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Swedish mathematician Arne Beurling broke the German Siemens T52 Geheimschreiber cipher in two weeks in 1940 — an achievement described by NSA historians as perhaps the greatest individual cryptanalytic feat in history.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Bible Code — Equidistant Letter Sequences","u":"ciphers/bible-code.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"1994 · Refuted 1999","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Debunked","s":"The Bible Code — equidistant letter sequences (ELS) claimed to hide prophecies in the Hebrew scriptures. A rigorous statistical refutation by McKay et al. (1999) showed the same method finds identical 'predictions' in War and Peace and Moby-Dick.","g":["digital","puzzle & novelty ciphers"]},{"t":"Bifid Cipher","u":"ciphers/bifid.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"1901","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Statistical analysis · Hill climbing","s":"Bifid cipher (1901) — Felix Delastelle's fractionation cipher that splits Polybius coordinates, transposes them across a period, and recombines.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Bill Tutte","u":"ciphers/bill-tutte.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Britain","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Bill Tutte was a Cambridge mathematician who reconstructed the complete logical structure of the Lorenz SZ40 cipher machine from ciphertext alone — without ever seeing the machine — enabling the creation of Colossus, the world's first programmable electronic computer.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Bletchley Park","u":"ciphers/bletchley-park.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · 1939–1945","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"","s":"Bletchley Park (Station X) was the British WWII signals intelligence headquarters where 10,000 people broke Enigma, Lorenz, and dozens of other Axis ciphers — arguably shortening the war by two years.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Book Cipher","u":"ciphers/book-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Field","c":"18th–20th c.","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Identifying the book — the Beale Cipher 2 was solved when the anonymous pamphleteer (published by James Ward, 1885) tried the Declaration of Independence","s":"Book Cipher (18th c.) — if both correspondents share the same book, the book itself is the key. Used by Benedict Arnold, Aaron Burr, and Beale.","g":["19th century","military & field","broken"]},{"t":"Cabinet Noir — Black Chambers of Europe","u":"ciphers/cabinet-noir.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Europe · 1550–1850","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"","s":"The European Black Chambers (cabinets noirs) were government departments that intercepted, deciphered, and resealed diplomatic mail from the 16th through 19th centuries — proto-signals-intelligence agencies.","g":["renaissance","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Caesar Cipher","u":"ciphers/caesar.html","k":"cipher","h":"Hall I","c":"Ancient Rome","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Frequency Analysis · Al-Kindi · 850 AD","s":"Caesar cipher — the simplest substitution cipher, used by Julius Caesar around 58 BC with a shift of 3. Broken by Al-Kindi's frequency analysis circa 850 AD.","g":["ancient","hall i","broken"]},{"t":"Cardano Autokey","u":"ciphers/cardano-autokey.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Polyalphabetic Era","c":"Italian Renaissance · 1550","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Easy (recovery cascade)","s":"Girolamo Cardano's 1550 autokey — prime the keystream with a single seed letter, then continue it with the plaintext itself. The first published self-keying scheme.","g":["renaissance","the polyalphabetic era","broken"]},{"t":"Cardano Grille","u":"ciphers/cardano-grille.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Transposition Hall","c":"Italian Renaissance · 1550","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Weak (steganography, not encryption)","s":"Girolamo Cardano's 1550 grille — a card with cut-out windows. Write the secret through the windows; fill the rest with innocuous text; the recipient overlays an identical grille to read.","g":["renaissance","the transposition hall","historical only"]},{"t":"Chaocipher","u":"ciphers/chaocipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Modern · 1918","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Published key-recovery attacks by Lasry, Rubin, Kopal and Wacker (2016).","s":"Chaocipher (1918) — John F. Byrne's two-disk dynamic cipher. Disclosed in 2010; published known-plaintext and in-depth ciphertext-only attacks recover its keys.","g":["wwi","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Che Guevara's VIC Variant","u":"ciphers/che-guevara.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Cold War · 1956–1967","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Bolivian Army capture (1967)","s":"Ernesto 'Che' Guevara used a modified VIC straddling-checkerboard cipher for guerrilla communications in Bolivia and the Congo, 1956–1967.","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Chinese Telegraph Code","u":"ciphers/chinese-telegraph.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Late Qing → PRC · 1881–1980s","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Public codebook (super-enciphered when secret)","s":"The 1881 Chinese Telegraph Code mapped 7,000+ Chinese characters to 4-digit code groups so they could ride the same Morse-style telegraph network as the Latin alphabet — and was super-enciphered for diplomatic and military traffic in the 20th century.","g":["modern","civil war gallery"]},{"t":"Cicada 3301","u":"ciphers/cicada-3301.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Internet era · 2012–2014","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Multi-stage ARG / recruitment puzzle","s":"An anonymous 2012–2014 internet recruitment puzzle that combined steganography, classical ciphers, GPS coordinates on physical posters, and a copy of the Liber Primus hand-illustrated in glyphic runes.","g":["digital","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Columnar Transposition","u":"ciphers/columnar.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"19th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Frequency analysis · Anagramming","s":"Columnar transposition — write plaintext under a keyword, then read columns in keyword order. Broken by Friedman-era multiple-anagram methods in the 1920s.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Commercial Telegraph Codebooks","u":"ciphers/commercial-codebooks.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Victorian → inter-war · 1870s–1930s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Public codebooks (compression, not secrecy)","s":"Commercial telegraph codebooks (ABC Code, Bentley’s, Lieber’s) were the workhorses of Victorian and Edwardian global business. Each English word or phrase mapped to a 5-letter pronounceable codeword — cheaper, denser, and incidentally a layer of confidentiality.","g":["modern","civil war gallery","broken"]},{"t":"Confederate Vigenère","u":"ciphers/confederate-vigenere.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Confederate · 1862","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Union cryptanalysts · Kasiski (published 1863)","s":"Confederate Vigenère (1861-1865) — the brass cipher disk used by Confederate officers, broken in 1863 once the three repeating field keys were recovered.","g":["wwi","civil war gallery","broken"]},{"t":"Copiale Cipher","u":"ciphers/copiale.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty","c":"18th Century · ~1760s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Knight, Megyesi, Schaefer (USC/Uppsala, 2011)","s":"Copiale Cipher — the 105-page 18th-century Oculist Order manuscript with homophonic substitution. Broken in 2011 by Knight, Megyesi, and Schaefer using HMM.","g":["renaissance","puzzle & novelty","broken"]},{"t":"Culper Ring / Tallmadge Code","u":"ciphers/culper-ring.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"American Revolution · 1778–1783","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Codebook (never captured, never broken)","s":"Major Benjamin Tallmadge’s 1779 codebook for George Washington’s Culper Spy Ring. Roughly 760 numbered entries covering people, places, military terms, and common words — used by Abraham Woodhull and Robert Townsend on British-occupied Long Island and Manhattan.","g":["modern","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"The Da Vinci Code","u":"ciphers/da-vinci-code.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Fiction · 2003","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Pop Culture","s":"Dan Brown's 2003 thriller made cryptography mainstream — Atbash, the Fibonacci sequence, and anagrams as plot devices that introduced millions to ci