n","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Anagramming · Hill climbing (difficult)","s":"Double transposition — apply columnar transposition twice with two keys. The WWII Allied workhorse, considered unbreakable until Lasry et al. (2013).","g":["wwi","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Elonka Dunin","u":"ciphers/dunin.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Modern · Cryptographer","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Living Legend","s":"Elonka Dunin is a game designer, cryptographer, and the foremost public authority on unsolved ciphers — the creator of the definitive list of famous unsolved codes and an expert on Kryptos.","g":["modern","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"ECDSA","u":"ciphers/ecdsa.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1992","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"","s":"ECDSA (1992) — signing with elliptic curves, and the one-line mistake that handed Sony's PlayStation 3 master key to the world.","g":["digital","modern cryptography","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"Egyptian Substitution Hieroglyphs","u":"ciphers/egyptian-substitution.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Middle Kingdom Egypt · ~1900 BCE","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Decorative concealment","s":"The Khnumhotep II tomb inscription (~1900 BCE) — the world's earliest surviving instance of a deliberate substitution cipher, using unusual hieroglyph variants.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography"]},{"t":"Elizebeth Smith Friedman","u":"ciphers/elizebeth-friedman.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Interwar & WWII · USA","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Elizebeth Smith Friedman was America's first female professional cryptanalyst, who broke rum-runner codes during Prohibition, exposed Nazi