sance · ~1400","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Statistical analysis · Structural pattern detection","s":"Homophonic substitution (1400s) — replace each letter with one of several numeric codes to flatten frequencies. Defeated Mary in 1586; Great Cipher in 1893.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"IRA Cipher Traffic","u":"ciphers/ira-ciphers.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Ireland · 1926–1928","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Jim Gillogly — hill climbing over column orders","s":"About 1,300 IRA cryptograms from the papers of chief of staff Moss Twomey, mostly 1926–1927, enciphered by columnar transposition and broken almost in their entirety by Jim Gillogly with hill-climbing software — published as Decoding the IRA (2008).","g":["interwar","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Jefferson Disk","u":"ciphers/jefferson-disk.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"1795 (Jefferson) · 1922 (M-94)","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Brute force · Probable word attack","s":"Jefferson disk cipher (1795) — Thomas Jefferson's 36-disk wheel cipher, rediscovered and adopted by the US Army as the M-94 in 1922. Solved by the late 20th c.","g":["interwar","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"JN-25","u":"ciphers/jn-25.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"World War II - 1939-1945","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Station HYPO and allied Pacific units","s":"JN-25 (1939-1945) - the Imperial Japanese Navy's superenciphered codebook system broken by Station HYPO and central to Midway intelligence.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Joan Clarke","u":"ciphers/joan-clarke.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Britain","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Joan Clarke was a Bletchley Park cryptanalyst who worked in Hut 8 alongside Alan Turing, making crucial contributions to breaking the Naval Enigma and to the development of the Bombe.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"David Kahn","u":"ciphers/kahn.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Modern · Journalist/Historian","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Cryptohistory Biography","s":"David Kahn's 1967 masterwork The Codebreakers was the first comprehensive history of cryptography — a book so thorough that the NSA tried to suppress its publication.","g":["modern","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Kama Sutra Cipher","u":"ciphers/kama-sutra.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Classical India · ~400 CE","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"","s":"The Mlecchita Vikalpa cipher from Vatsyayana's Kama Sutra (~400 CE) — a paired-letter substitution taught to lovers as one of the 64 arts.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Kerckhoffs's Principle","u":"ciphers/kerckhoffs.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Foundational · 1883","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Principle (not a cipher)","s":"Auguste Kerckhoffs's 1883 principle — 'A cipher should be secure even if everything about the system, except the key, is public knowledge' — is the bedrock of modern cryptographic design.","g":["19th century","modern cryptography"]},{"t":"KL-7 ADONIS","u":"ciphers/kl-7.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Cold War · 1952","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Never publicly broken by cryptanalysis in operational service; stolen keys exposed traffic.","s":"The KL-7 (ADONIS) was NATO’s standard cipher machine from 1952 to 1968. Eight rotors, seven of which stepped, on a re-entrant alphabet. Compromised by John Walker, not by cryptanalysis.","g":["cold war","mechanical cipher machines","historical only"]},{"t":"Kryha","u":"ciphers/kryha.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Inter-war commercial · 1924","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Broken (Friedman, 1933)","s":"Alexander von Kryha’s 1924 pocket cipher — a clockwork-driven mixed alphabet wheel sold to banks and diplomats as unbreakable, then broken in two hours by William Friedman.","g":["interwar","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"ARG Ciphers","u":"ciphers/krypto-arg.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Web 2.0 · 2001–present","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Promotional / collaborative cipher hunts","s":"Alternate-reality games — from The Beast (2001) to Year Zero (2007) and beyond — built on the assumption that players will recognise, transcribe, and crack classical ciphers across forums, phone calls, and physical artifacts.","g":["digital","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Kryptos","u":"ciphers/kryptos.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Modern · 1990","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"K1–K3 Broken · K4 Unsolved","s":"Kryptos (1990) — Jim Sanborn's CIA-courtyard sculpture in four sections. K1, K2, K3 solved by 1999 (keyed Vigenère and transposition); K4 still unsolved.","g":["digital","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"George Lasry","u":"ciphers/lasry.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Modern · Computational","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Living Legend","s":"George Lasry is a computational cryptographer who has solved more historically unsolved cipher manuscripts in the past decade than anyone alive — including the Copiale Cipher and multiple Zodiac Killer messages.","g":["modern","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Latin American Telegraphic Codebooks","u":"ciphers/latin-american-codebooks.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Americas · 1870s–1940s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Low (commercial, not military)","s":"The commercial telegraph boom of 1870–1940 spawned a rich family of Latin American codebooks — from the Código Comercial Mexicano to the Código Telegráfico Argentino — that shaped 20th-century trade.","g":["modern","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Leo Marks","u":"ciphers/leo-marks.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Britain","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Leo Marks was the SOE's head of cryptography in WWII, who replaced vulnerable poem codes with one-time pads for field agents, dramatically reducing agent capture rates. He wrote the poem 'The Life That I Have' for agent Violette Szabo.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Lorenz Cipher","u":"ciphers/lorenz.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"WWII · Germany · 1940","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Bill Tutte · Known plaintext · Colossus computer (1944)","s":"Lorenz SZ40 cipher (1940) — the German High Command teleprinter cipher with twelve pin-wheels. Broken by Bill Tutte in 1942; industrialised by Colossus in 1944.","g":["wwii","the unbreakable","broken"]},{"t":"M-94 / CSP-488","u":"ciphers/m-94.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"WWI → WWII tactical · 1922–1943","b":"Broken","trust