20th-c. United States · 1999","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Unsolved homicide-evidence cipher","s":"Two handwritten notes found in the pockets of murder victim Ricky McCormick in 1999 — declared 'unbreakable' by the FBI's Cryptanalysis and Racketeering Records Unit in 2011.","g":["digital","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Microdot Steganography","u":"ciphers/microdot.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Modern · 1941–1980s","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Physical discovery — the FBI photographically enlarged suspicious punctuation","s":"The microdot — a photograph reduced to the size of a typographic period — was the most effective covert communication tool of the 20th century, used by the Abwehr, KGB, and CIA from WWII through the Cold War.","g":["wwii","military & spy ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"MIT Mystery Hunt","u":"ciphers/mit-mystery-hunt.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"MIT · 1981–present","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Annual collaborative puzzle hunt","s":"An annual three-day puzzle marathon at MIT (since 1981) where teams solve dozens of layered cryptographic, linguistic, and lateral-thinking puzzles to find a hidden coin somewhere on campus.","g":["cold war","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Monoalphabetic Substitution","u":"ciphers/monoalphabetic.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Medieval · ~800 AD","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Al-Kindi · Frequency Analysis · 850 AD","s":"Monoalphabetic substitution — replace each letter with a fixed substitute. Foundation of all substitution ciphers; broken by Al-Kindi's frequency analysis ~850.","g":["medieval","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Morse Code","u":"ciphers/morse.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Telegraph age · 1840s →","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Encoding, not encryption","s":"International Morse code — the dot-dash telegraph alphabet that made the wired world possible and provided the carrier signal for a century of military and amateur ciphers.","g":["victorian","world origins of cryptography"]},{"t":"Myszkowski Transposition","u":"ciphers/myszkowski.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"1902 · France","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Repeated-keyword transposition preserves letter frequencies and is vulnerable to statistical and keyword-search attacks.","s":"Myszkowski transposition (1902) — repeated keyword letters share a rank. Encrypt and decrypt interactively, including incomplete final rows, and compare columnar transposition.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"National Treasure","u":"ciphers/national-treasure.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Fiction · 2004","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Pop Culture","s":"The 2004 Disney film National Treasure sent audiences hunting for cipher clues on the US dollar bill, the Liberty Bell, and Independence Hall — all real historical sites.","g":["digital","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Navajo Code Talkers","u":"ciphers/navajo-code-talkers.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"WWII · 1942–1945","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Never publicly broken in wartime service; a historical military code.","s":"Navajo Code (1942-1945) — a constructed military vocabulary spoken by 420 Marine Code Talkers. Never broken in wartime; declassified in 1968 after long use.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","historical only"]},{"t":"NEMA — Swiss Rotor Machine","u":"ciphers/nema.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Postwar Switzerland · 1947","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Obsolete Swiss military rotor machine. The demo fixes one historical wheel configuration; the 17,576-keypress state period is not its key space.","s":"Swiss NEMA rotor machine (1947) — four cipher wheels, independent stepping wheels, and a moving reflector. Explore historical military encryption and decryption.","g":["cold war","mechanical cipher machines","historical only"]},{"t":"Nihilist Cipher","u":"ciphers/nihilist.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"1880s · Russia","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Frequency analysis of digit pairs · Kasiski-equivalent","s":"Nihilist cipher (1880s) — the Russian revolutionary classic adding Polybius numbers to a repeating keyword's Polybius numbers by plain addition. Broken early 1900s.","g":["19th century","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Nomenclator","u":"ciphers/nomenclator.html","k":"cipher","h":"Substitution Era","c":"Renaissance · 1400s–1800s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Black Chambers (Vienna, Paris, London) c. 1700–1850 by sheer intercept volume","s":"Nomenclator (1400s) — The 400-year diplomatic compromise: a small substitution alphabet plus a large code dictionary. Hall II of The Cipher Museum.","g":["renaissance","substitution era","broken"]},{"t":"Null Cipher","u":"ciphers/null-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Transposition Hall","c":"Antiquity → present","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Weak (concealment, not encryption)","s":"The null cipher — hide the secret in plain sight by spelling it out with the first letters of an innocuous message. Used by spies, prisoners, and Bacon, since at least the Roman empire.","g":["ancient","the transposition hall","historical only"]},{"t":"One-Time Pad","u":"ciphers/one-time-pad.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"1882 · Theoretical","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"Requires a secret, truly random, message-length pad used once.","s":"One-Time Pad (Miller 1882 / Mauborgne 1917) — the only cipher mathematically unbreakable when the key is truly random, key-length, and never reused (Shannon).","g":["theoretical","the unbreakable","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"Patterson's Cipher for Jefferson","u":"ciphers/patterson-jefferson-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"1801","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Solved 2007 — after 206 years","s":"Robert Patterson's 1801 cipher for Thomas Jefferson — text written down the lines, cut into sections of at most nine, the lines shuffled and padded with arbitrary letters. Unread for 206 years until Lawren Smithline solved it in 2007 by dynamic programming over digraph frequencies.","g":["modern","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Phaistos Disc","u":"ciphers/phaistos-disc.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Bronze Age Crete · ~1700 BCE","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Undeciphered script","s":"A 1700 BCE Cretan terracotta disc stamped with 45 distinct pictograms — the world's oldest 'movable type' and one of its most enduring undeciphered scripts.","g":["ancient","unsolved ciphers"]},{"t":"Pigpen Cipher","u":"ciphers/pigpen.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"18th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Symbol frequency analysis · Pattern recognition","s":"Pigpen cipher (~1700) — Freemason geometric substitution where each letter becomes the lines and dots of its grid cell. Trivial once the glyph table is seen.","g":["medieval","puzzle & novelty ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Playfair Cipher","u":"ciphers/playfair.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Victorian · 1854","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Digraph statistics · hill climbing","s":"Playfair cipher (1854) — Charles Wheatstone's keyed 5x5 digram cipher promoted by Lord Playfair. Used by the British in WWI; broken by digram analysis.","g":["19th century","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Polybius Square","u":"ciphers/polybius.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Ancient Greece · ~150 BC","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Frequency analysis","s":"Polybius square (~150 BC) — ancient Greek 5x5 grid mapping each letter to a row-column digit pair. Foundation for Bifid, Trifid, Nihilist, ADFGX, and tap codes.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Porta Cipher","u":"ciphers/porta.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Revolution","c":"Renaissance · 1563","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Index of Coincidence · Statistical analysis","s":"Porta cipher (1563) — della Porta's reciprocal polyalphabetic table where each key letter swaps two halves of the alphabet. Falls to Kasiski just like Vigenère.","g":["renaissance","polyalphabetic revolution","broken"]},{"t":"Purple (97-shiki ōbun injiki)","u":"ciphers/purple.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"World War II · 1939–1945","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Frank Rowlett, Genevieve Grotjan & the US SIS · 20 September 1940","s":"Purple machine (1939) — Japan's Type 97 stepping-switch cipher for diplomatic traffic. Broken on 20 September 1940 by Frank Rowlett and the US Army SIS team.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Rail Fence Cipher","u":"ciphers/rail-fence.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"19th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Brute force · Trivial keyspace","s":"Rail Fence cipher — write the message in a zigzag across N rails, then read off rail by rail. Trivial to break: brute-force every rail count from 2 to about 10.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Red Army Faction One-Time Pad","u":"ciphers/red-army-faction.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Cold War · 1970–1998","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"","s":"The West German Red Army Faction (RAF/Baader-Meinhof) used KGB-supplied one-time pads for communications — materials discovered in 1993 after German reunification archive access.","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Red (Type A)","u":"ciphers/red-type-a.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Interwar to WWII - 1931-1938","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Broken by SIS","s":"Red (Type A) (1931-1938) - the Japanese diplomatic stepping-switch predecessor to Purple and the cryptanalytic bridge to MAGIC.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Joseph Rochefort","u":"ciphers/rochefort.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Pacific","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Commander Joseph Rochefort led Station HYPO and broke JN-25 in 1942, providing the intelligence that won the Battle of Midway and turned the tide of the Pacific War.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"The Rosetta Stone","u":"ciphers/rosetta-stone.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Ptolemaic Egypt · 196 BCE","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Solved 1822 (Champollion)","s":"The Rosetta Stone (196 BCE) — the trilingual decree that unlocked Egyptian hieroglyphs after fourteen centuries of silence, deciphered by Champollion in 1822.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography"]},{"t":"ROT13","u":"ciphers/rot13.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1980s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Knowing the system","s":"ROT13 (~1983, Usenet) — the self-inverse Caesar with shift 13, designed as a spoiler obscurer rather than a cipher. One operation toggles text in and out.","g":["19th century","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"RSA","u":"ciphers/rsa.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1977","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"","s":"The first practical public-key cryptosystem — one key encrypts, a different key decrypts, and you can publish the encrypting key in the phone book.","g":["cold war","modern cryptography","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"Running Key Cipher","u":"ciphers/running-key.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Revolution","c":"19th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Source text identification · Statistical analysis","s":"Running Key cipher (19th c.) — a Vigenère variant using a long passage of natural text as the key. Broken by Friedman with high-probability-trigram dragging.","g":["19th century","polyalphabetic revolution","broken"]},{"t":"Sator Square","u":"ciphers/sator-square.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Roman Empire · 1st–4th c. CE","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Palindromic word square / hidden acrostic","s":"A 1st-century Roman five-letter palindromic word square — SATOR · AREPO · TENET · OPERA · ROTAS — readable forwards, backwards, up, and down. Every element of the square also rearranges into PATER NOSTER + A and O.","g":["ancient","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Scytale","u":"ciphers/scytale.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Sparta · ~500 BC","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Brute force — try different rod diameters","s":"Scytale (~500 BC) — the Spartan transposition device that wraps a leather strip around a rod of fixed diameter. Broken trivially by trying every rod width.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"SHA-256","u":"ciphers/sha256.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 2001","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"","s":"SHA-256 (2001) — a 256-bit fingerprint for any input; the verification engine of Git, Bitcoin, TLS certificates, and software integrity worldwide.","g":["digital","modern cryptography","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"Shamir's Secret Sharing","u":"ciphers/shamir-secret-sharing.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1979","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"","s":"Shamir's Secret Sharing (1979) — split a secret so any k of n holders can rebuild it and any k−1 learn absolutely nothing.","g":["cold war","modern cryptography","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"Shugborough Inscription","u":"ciphers/shugborough.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Georgian England · ~1748–1763","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Unsolved monument inscription","s":"An 18th-century English garden monument bearing the cryptic letters O · U · O · S · V · A · V · V — debated for 250 years and never definitively cracked.","g":["modern","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"SIGABA (ECM Mark II)","u":"ciphers/sigaba.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Machines","c":"WWII · 1940","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Never publicly broken by cryptanalysis in wartime service; obsolete today.","s":"SIGABA (ECM Mark II) (1940) — The American WWII rotor machine the Axis never broke — fifteen rotors, irregular stepping. Hall VII of The Cipher Museum.","g":["wwii","mechanical machines","historical only"]},{"t":"SIGSALY — The Encrypted Speech System","u":"ciphers/sigsaly.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"WWII · 1943–1946","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"","s":"SIGSALY (1943–1946) was the world's first perfectly secure voice encryption system — a 50-ton machine using vinyl phonograph records as one-time pads to protect Roosevelt-Churchill phone calls during WWII.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","historical only"]},{"t":"Slidex","u":"ciphers/slidex.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"WWII & Cold War · 1943–1980s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Tactical (intentionally weak)","s":"Slidex (1943) — the British Army's front-line tactical code card. A 204-cell vocabulary grid plus two sliding coordinate strips. Fast for a platoon signaller; read by German units within hours.","g":["wwii","civil war gallery","broken"]},{"t":"Solitaire / Pontifex","u":"ciphers/solitaire.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"Modern · 1999","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"","s":"Solitaire / Pontifex (1999) — Bruce Schneier's hand cipher driven by a 54-card deck (written for Cryptonomicon). Crowley showed a ~1/22.5 keystream bias.","g":["digital","the unbreakable","historical only"]},{"t":"Somerton Man Code","u":"ciphers/somerton-man.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Post-WWII Australia · 1948","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Unsolved Cold-War-era inscription","s":"An unidentified man found dead on an Adelaide beach in 1948 with the word 'Tamám Shud' in his pocket — and a page of letters that may or may not be a cipher.","g":["wwii","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Stager Cipher","u":"ciphers/stager.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Union · 1861","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Never broken in wartime — superseded after the war","s":"Stager cipher (1861) — Anson Stager's Union Army route cipher that