f the Bifid that fractionates letters into trits and shuffles them across a period.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Trithemius Progressive Cipher","u":"ciphers/trithemius.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Polyalphabetic Era","c":"German Renaissance · 1518","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Easy","s":"Johannes Trithemius's progressive shift cipher (Polygraphia, 1518) — the first published polyalphabetic, where every successive letter is shifted one further than the last.","g":["renaissance","the polyalphabetic era","broken"]},{"t":"Fleissner Turning Grille","u":"ciphers/turning-grille.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"1881 · German WWI use","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"A transposition cipher: quarter-turn holes permute a complete block, preserving letter frequencies.","s":"Fleissner turning grille — a transposition cipher used by Germany in WWI. Rotate an interactive mask 90 degrees and contrast it with Cardano steganography.","g":["wwi","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Two-Square Cipher","u":"ciphers/two-square.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Late 19th Century · ~1902","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Digram frequency · same-row leak","s":"Two-Square cipher (late 19th c.) — a lighter Delastelle variant using only two keyed 5x5 squares. Many digrams self-encode, leaking the key squares.","g":["19th century","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Typex","u":"ciphers/typex.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Machines","c":"WWII · 1937","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Never publicly broken by cryptanalysis in wartime service; obsolete today.","s":"Typex (1937) — Britain's improved Enigma — five rotors, no plugboard, used at Bletchley to read its own intercepts. Hall VII of The Cipher Museum.","g":["wwii","mechanical machines","historical only"]},{"t":"VENONA Project","u":"ciphers/venona.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"Cold War · 1943–1980","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Partial break (key reuse exploit)","s":"VENONA (1943–1980) was the US Army codebreaking effort that decrypted thousands of Soviet intelligence cables by exploiting key reuse in one-time pads — revealing Julius Rosenberg, Kim Philby, and the Cambridge Five.","g":["wwii","the unbreakable","broken"]},{"t":"Vernam Cipher","u":"ciphers/vernam.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"1917","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"Secure only under OTP conditions: a secret, truly random, message-length pad used once. XOR alone or pad reuse is not secure.","s":"Vernam cipher (1917) — Gilbert Vernam's AT&T XOR teleprinter patent, the direct ancestor of the One-Time Pad and every modern stream cipher including ChaCha20.","g":["wwi","the unbreakable","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"VIC Cipher","u":"ciphers/vic.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Cold War · 1950s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Never broken by cryptanalysis — exposed by defector (1957)","s":"VIC cipher (1953) — Reino Häyhänen's KGB hand cipher: straddling checkerboard, double transposition, date-driven keystream. Broken in 1957 after his defection.","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Vietnamese Underground Codes","u":"ciphers/vietnamese-underground.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Modern · 1940s–1975","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"SIGINT (NSA/ARVN), captured materials","s":"The Viet Minh and NLF used a system of keyed monoalphabetic substitution and prearranged signal codes during the Indochina and Vietnam Wars (1940s–1975).","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Vigenère Cipher","u":"ciphers/vigenere.html","k":"cipher","h":"Hall III","c":"Renaissance · 1553","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Babbage ~1854 · Kasiski 1863","s":"Vigenère cipher (1553) — the polyalphabetic shift driven by a repeating keyword, called le chiffre indéchiffrable for 300 years. Broken by Kasiski in 1863.","g":["renaissance","hall iii","broken"]},{"t":"The Voynich Manuscript","u":"ciphers/voynich.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Early 15th c. · Carbon-dated 1404–1438","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"No one. Every published \"solution\" has been refuted.","s":"Voynich Manuscript (~1400s) — a 240-page illustrated codex in an unknown script (Beinecke MS 408). Has resisted cipher and linguistic analysis for 600 years.","g":["renaissance","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Wadsworth Cipher","u":"ciphers/wadsworth-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"1817 · USA","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Ciphertext-only stochastic hill climbing (2020)","s":"Wadsworth's cipher device (1817) — Colonel Decius Wadsworth's two geared disks, 33 outer positions against 26 inner letters, the first working progressive cipher machine. Broken by a ciphertext-only hill-climbing attack in 2020.","g":["19th century","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Wallis Ciphers","u":"ciphers/wallis-ciphers.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"English Civil War · 1642–1651","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Royalist nomenclators — broken by Wallis","s":"John Wallis of Oxford broke Royalist nomenclators for Parliament during the English Civil War, then served every English government for 50 years. Originator of professional English state cryptanalysis.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Wheatstone Cryptograph","u":"ciphers/wheatstone.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Victorian London · 1867","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Weak (1867 standards)","s":"Charles Wheatstone's 1867 cryptograph — two geared clock dials whose hands sweep an outer plain alphabet and an inner mixed alphabet, generating a polyalphabetic ciphertext one click at a time.","g":["victorian","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Herbert Yardley","u":"ciphers/yardley.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Interwar · USA","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Herbert Osborne Yardley founded America's Black Chamber in 1919, broke Japanese diplomatic codes at the 1921 Washington Naval Conference, and then infamously published the secrets in his 1931 bestseller.","g":["interwar","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Zero-Knowledge Proofs","u":"ciphers/zero-knowledge-proofs.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1985","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","t